#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "2efe9f75-18c0-5a86-b56b-3f338b7e5c2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1702" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the graph of the straight line below?\n\nI. $y=2x-1$\n\nII. $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1$\n\nIII. $2x-y=1$\n\n![](images/93/93dc9d447c15e4597549996b1f0b533cf86783b5cc5df032a4c46d4ca8934abc.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, we can choose $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-1)$ and $(2,3)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/a9/a9f18453dedb1763908825301f80ef86307d3a3aceaf678bbe8579a42a0996f5.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=2x-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & =2(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =-1\\,✓ \\\\ (3) & =2(2)-1 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2x-1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =-1\\,✓ \\\\ (3) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2)-1 \\\\ 3 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $2x-y=1.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-(-1) & =1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓ \\\\ 2(2)-(3) & =1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2x-y=1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that represented the line in the graph were $y=2x-1$ and $2x-y=1,$ corresponding to answer choices I and III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3123f8ed-a1ca-52b7-8427-2f3b60d9e68a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Lie on a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "92c02155-ad64-59a7-be4c-052e5bdfb429" :question/math-academy-id "e-6096" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f87e0eb91b6ec47ca8a4d64c2824396e613123ed37862975b606608ff70445f0.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=5-2x.$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5), B(2,1)$ and $C(3,0).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5-2x \\\\ (5) & =5-2(0) \\\\ 5 & =5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line:\n\n- Substituting the point $B(2,1)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5-2x \\\\ (1) & =5-2(2) \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line:\n\n- Substituting the point $C(3,0)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5-2x \\\\ (0) & =5-2(3) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only points $A$ and $B$ lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2491cf6c-260b-5992-a910-9fe6a80e30bc" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Line Using a Table"}] :question/id #uuid "6b16ff95-8b78-587d-a7c8-8d0909b69f19" :question/math-academy-id "e-983" :question/problem "Use the table below to plot the graph of $y=3x-2.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=3x-2.$\nTherefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=3(0)-2=-2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=3(1)-2=1,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=3(2)-2=4,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=3(3)-2=7.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/09/09e670353b2114b229769e2bd6b3eb4a9895389b818fc3f4ace6997a3e7efa3f.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=3x-2.$\n\n![](images/d2/d2d8891102ce99020f544bd8d82a5ff0454080474ce4267ef5285db92f5b2ca0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebeb4e5a-adbe-5a94-b05d-61933a687892" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Line Using Two Points"}] :question/id #uuid "4aad1578-4134-5f25-88cb-8ab4631257ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-984" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\nIt is often easiest to pick one of the points to have an $x$-coordinate of $x=0.$ So, for the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+3 \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,3).$\n\nFor the second point, let's choose $x=4.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(4)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(4,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+3.$\n\n![](images/36/36689081acaacb24a09826c3927388697e77d92caeca900d053d33d0f99d2b8c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebeb4e5a-adbe-5a94-b05d-61933a687892" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Line Using Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81fe708f-9b1b-5b89-a97e-75b73434d8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b27fed5fef04aced7d8961bdcb9e4e46a20fef45ca409c1431b3694f956583dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489811} {:answer/id #uuid "aa270835-7b14-5b5a-b1ea-ade934a087c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/484ae151bb70dcd08f488bbc1b6912f8b6cf9db9f9b7befb953585fb83ea164b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489889} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a4d489-41a8-5dcf-8696-da067c363924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9c4d21e0fdbdb082c1efdd50936da2cd8c131be9b5ac3fa0fa85a73f8c11cf48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489978} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6bc548-b54d-5ef3-9f0e-1da8be1a265c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b2bb0b6f019af476eae3e76adb7ac2ef4745a6c4bdeae24cd4a40320b23f882b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490024} {:answer/id #uuid "fba812d1-3f67-596b-b851-fa0314c2cb48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6aa0661b20e4d114abb475dfc3cba6180bed5155c6051262a68c31110408a18.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb6bc548-b54d-5ef3-9f0e-1da8be1a265c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b2bb0b6f019af476eae3e76adb7ac2ef4745a6c4bdeae24cd4a40320b23f882b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490024} :answer-field/id #uuid "d17d82c6-1a23-5c4e-bb97-55ab46a58407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f93db21-c74e-5e95-a404-6814f4df05d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-102054" :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-2x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-2x.$ \n\n![](images/c6/c632cc2770958b5d73f6e143de0b72d19b2099f9f656ff34f273d88acc4f9cb4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3123f8ed-a1ca-52b7-8427-2f3b60d9e68a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Lie on a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f20f4e-40c9-5a9d-8129-6ea89406c9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489592} {:answer/id #uuid "2e314e93-a74f-58a2-8a61-c6709c1e6a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489617} {:answer/id #uuid "2e61ea38-9adb-5df3-9e77-c49b744c8375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489618} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cdeb7e-9f2c-5249-a813-a2de4fe3188f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489951} {:answer/id #uuid "d311c0e5-165f-5e28-b3c6-245a633be859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e314e93-a74f-58a2-8a61-c6709c1e6a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489617} :answer-field/id #uuid "73f7dd8a-46cc-50f8-8f6c-5dc06aa45a32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3250f349-fbfe-56f8-96e2-52cb212003b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-102142" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dad50d17444096c60b129131104209b8b7371d546f80d7b0c838baec1df8b4d.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-5x+6?$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5),B(1,2)$ and $C(2,-4).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (5) & =-5(0)+6 \\\\ 5 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does *not* lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (2) & =-5(1)+6 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (-4) & =-5(2)+6 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebeb4e5a-adbe-5a94-b05d-61933a687892" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Line Using Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1395c3db-95d9-5639-b9d4-30e6fc772906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0cfc4bba4867b40c4c38b0886c37bb5c4cd64d293530ece7c67cdb6349134013.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489558} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2e4dbc-417e-567d-87f0-6c258efaf698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9e2977ee1faa5dde2d1bf3a427037aac75b2692486bdcb724356452765c8b661.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489648} {:answer/id #uuid "73631664-2efe-5676-8831-b04bad13e778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/0678cd88949fac3a9ce58d255eee9d8a07ba8c3b91a86f90fa24d70d1510568f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489784} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5037ef-50db-500f-ac5f-db15fcc0b6b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/24/24d776784e54d7e38452f9b548051d0a2015e119f7c701ac74be87ef3dbb42f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489794} {:answer/id #uuid "c346fa15-1ab9-5397-875e-59d3c4e305eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/afb86fd4a7ffd591dfbbc6e8ee837b33b943972212b04b15f9040b310b0abdba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73631664-2efe-5676-8831-b04bad13e778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/0678cd88949fac3a9ce58d255eee9d8a07ba8c3b91a86f90fa24d70d1510568f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489784} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ceda208-3258-5df9-a5ee-0311a1f10448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5b958d4-e62e-522f-9fd3-554270dc7fdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109055" :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=8.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(8) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(8,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8c562212687c848e2de25b757f59acd70467a085ae2e0713fbeafa355ad5bb5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026cf452-bbce-5666-b06e-eea33f9d7832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489517} {:answer/id #uuid "9acb1b55-d961-5760-970a-55e90ab17c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489858} {:answer/id #uuid "c834e69d-11f0-589f-aef2-acdc449ab184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489948} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f1f968-d792-57b5-9f02-cbac084ac726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489956} {:answer/id #uuid "e524c8b2-8988-581d-b06e-10a171578329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "026cf452-bbce-5666-b06e-eea33f9d7832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489517} :answer-field/id #uuid "44f33cfa-5885-5a2f-90fc-d69e3d361204" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a635937b-674b-54dd-a770-48fe7b870be8" :question/math-academy-id "q-54293" :question/problem "![](images/71/716836dd3dfc93ac26bf9a960d5edcd04da96907b98c39e926a917b7b8feeeee.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nII. $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,2)$ and $(4,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2b/2b9174d86d123228ae24fffeda4d2e4741e4a776f6671f7eff38340a59ba4d40.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-1(0)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =-2+2 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =2+2 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2491cf6c-260b-5992-a910-9fe6a80e30bc" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Line Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95871ea8-427c-5d8b-b7b9-90621fa03a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6a26ee2942f083a2edb99e9839d71577e1772bf6410e1ae4beafcde7f59c7109.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053458} {:answer/id #uuid "c7240b34-1c9b-5ca5-9452-e210e669f37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/893dba533e0afffe05f9603293355ccf38b2723fcd91608ea03fe321e26a0d5f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053459} {:answer/id #uuid "17588e8d-da64-5d6b-ab34-45af1c4a7804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/eaf735f72849b82e92f7baf0d991f3e024e013b66d9813d4e0d5f3a87632c901.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053460} {:answer/id #uuid "27023196-b279-5485-8ce7-7bf4dc0c7ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/afb9d602e1bff2fb643f44ed5dfa09d41833740e814739c60d5dd890a2f02270.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053461} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8ce376-c015-5edf-afdd-204f6ccb159f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/02f0d3d8bb1bd1e87d05e63579e2a92281de1d000fe2baa7c9942dd80b542efb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7240b34-1c9b-5ca5-9452-e210e669f37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/893dba533e0afffe05f9603293355ccf38b2723fcd91608ea03fe321e26a0d5f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053459} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bbe1741-021b-5eb6-8d9e-91ce5412b307" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4524558d-8a83-5843-a336-69f66463c3a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-54397" :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=x-2.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=x-2.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=(0)-2=-2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=(1)-2=-1,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=(2)-2=0,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=(3)-2=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/4f/4fc0e4386dd956c2b98c844941ce2e27cd1c9ca8578aca4cf5142e6971911831.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=x-2.$\n\n![](images/89/893dba533e0afffe05f9603293355ccf38b2723fcd91608ea03fe321e26a0d5f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2491cf6c-260b-5992-a910-9fe6a80e30bc" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Line Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3946c953-9228-5655-b35b-fd707ee85059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/5000be6af7e62672ecacf8fe90aa51286f459dd47f4b44c89c0e5c9fbfff84ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489642} {:answer/id #uuid "41956628-242f-5bff-a74b-494fa731d555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/497d1c67fb3a322ae402f6dfa69c8b069d91177ddef6a5847e6dbee4cb93f995.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489664} {:answer/id #uuid "a20e0b6e-d6c8-5746-bf51-509b5c8a9a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/18/18d9c0adc10639219feaca56d61376d5be64b5b49535c2a92a55f6e5d3422832.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489875} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe1a0f0-9239-5016-8995-7682b6c6771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/0400a1437f26b74feb16161cf51a88c175ee6837fe1e5d8f8785ba2bb2b9c68b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489932} {:answer/id #uuid "d90bc894-c41c-5fae-ad3b-1cd61eefe589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cc/cc985bf7790c1e8b6ec01ecc4b9bcde1c47073591ce02eef867ed652632a819c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfe1a0f0-9239-5016-8995-7682b6c6771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/0400a1437f26b74feb16161cf51a88c175ee6837fe1e5d8f8785ba2bb2b9c68b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489932} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a58be1d-9b02-526d-a31b-cb5a653e1224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60940cd3-550d-521c-8bf6-1906baac5be6" :question/math-academy-id "q-71695" :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x+2.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x+2.$\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2(0)+2=2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=2(1)+2=4,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2(2)+2=6,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)+2=8.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](images/6b/6b2da4bfc7c768788d143999fb61e76bb3a60facffc35e430d98085b395932f7.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of  $y=2x+2.$\n\n![](images/04/0400a1437f26b74feb16161cf51a88c175ee6837fe1e5d8f8785ba2bb2b9c68b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3123f8ed-a1ca-52b7-8427-2f3b60d9e68a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Lie on a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4f3252-d422-5b2b-87b7-6916c51a12a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489550} {:answer/id #uuid "38679d57-742c-5817-a31c-101bf33ca332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489640} {:answer/id #uuid "685aeab1-eebd-534e-8e4a-d21630e148b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489763} {:answer/id #uuid "a111be60-4cf1-5eab-b12f-bf275dc291fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489871} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fd167a-76c2-572d-ac6f-8214ea48c14a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f4f3252-d422-5b2b-87b7-6916c51a12a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489550} :answer-field/id #uuid "29e6c0ec-30d3-5bc5-89e9-08b3f00c0259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acf0d12e-cf58-5db7-b66f-2999c47f2d4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71829" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c33968d9b5c86e39ba35c88a5f28bb8950178004c763a5c011633b9a060e98a8.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=4x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,-2)$, $B(0,1)$ and $C(1,6).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (-2) & =4(-1)+2 \\\\ -2 & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (1) & =4(0)+2 \\\\ 1 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,6)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (6) & =4(1)+2 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce4d23e-eb28-56bb-bb93-3733846a6e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489585} {:answer/id #uuid "59fbb6ed-802e-5fc7-981b-ad071fe8f05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489735} {:answer/id #uuid "ad32efea-57f2-50ab-8978-d4988074759d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489894} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d8e2ce-cf6e-51e6-a3d1-b415eae5cfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489933} {:answer/id #uuid "e108ba0b-b8a5-543a-a12b-2f22efb52a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad32efea-57f2-50ab-8978-d4988074759d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489894} :answer-field/id #uuid "8571c77b-dc56-5521-b8ff-cf319f34e7c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d3bda62-1eef-5cb9-886b-004877c9a204" :question/math-academy-id "q-71837" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f886e9f5dfef693d1ea5d5a0a907a886be368549c1b9a4d43dce257d070e2fa2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y+3x=6$\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$\n\nIII. $y=-3x+6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,6)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/d6/d6cb6d026c181d79bda5c809729eb070c554c2025a287577df0330d1dd8a03e7.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y+3x=6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6)+3(0) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓ \\\\ (0)+3(2) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+3x=6$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(6)+(0) & =2 \\\\ 2+0 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(0)+(2) & =2 \\\\ 0+2 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-3x+6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6) & =-3(0)+6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-3(2)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-6+6 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3x+6$ is  an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nAll the given equations represented the line in the graph. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""}]}
